WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.875 --> 00:00:07.003
You know what's a lot harder
than it seems like it should be?

00:00:07.546 --> 00:00:09.839
Actually feeling alive.

00:00:10.632 --> 00:00:15.011
And what I mean by that
is that we are all constantly doing,

00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:17.264
or, at least, we're constantly scrolling.

00:00:17.305 --> 00:00:19.683
But we're not necessarily living.

00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:23.395
You know, we keep ourselves busy
to the point of exhaustion,

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:25.146
but we're also languishing.

00:00:25.146 --> 00:00:27.566
We feel a little bid dead inside.

00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:29.484
And I think we know that, on some level.

00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:31.152
I think that's part of the reason

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:33.822
we keep ourselves so busy
and distracted to begin with.

00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:36.074
But we don't know what to do about it.

00:00:36.491 --> 00:00:40.370
So I'm here to tell you
I figured out what to do about it.

00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:44.249
We need to have more fun.

00:00:44.874 --> 00:00:46.334
(Laughter)

00:00:46.334 --> 00:00:49.963
(Cheers and applause)

00:00:50.005 --> 00:00:53.717
So you might think that you're already
having plenty of fun,

00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:55.760
and that's because in our everyday speech,

00:00:55.802 --> 00:01:00.056
we often use the word "fun" to describe
anything we do with our leisure time,

00:01:00.098 --> 00:01:03.643
even if it's not actually enjoyable,
and, in fact, a waste of time.

00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:08.565
So for example, we scroll
through social media "for fun,"

00:01:08.607 --> 00:01:13.570
even though doing so often makes us feel
bad about, like, kind of everything.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.824
Or we'll say, "That was so fun.
We should do that again soon" --

00:01:17.866 --> 00:01:20.952
(Laughter)

00:01:20.994 --> 00:01:23.288
... in response to things
that weren't that fun

00:01:23.288 --> 00:01:26.458
and that we don't want to do again, ever.

00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:28.501
(Laughter)

00:01:28.543 --> 00:01:31.379
But it's not really our fault
that we're a little bit sloppy

00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:33.006
about how we use the word "fun,"

00:01:33.048 --> 00:01:35.675
because even the dictionary
doesn't get it quite right.

00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:38.887
It says that fun
is amusement or enjoyment,

00:01:38.887 --> 00:01:40.472
or lighthearted pleasure.

00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:43.433
It's something for kids
to have in play areas.

00:01:43.475 --> 00:01:46.645
It makes it sound
like it's frivolous and optional.

00:01:48.021 --> 00:01:50.482
But if you think back on your own memories

00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:53.485
that stand out to you
as having truly been fun --

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:55.445
and I really encourage you to do this --

00:01:55.487 --> 00:01:57.530
the memories that you would describe as --

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.824
and forgive me
for scientific terminology --

00:01:59.866 --> 00:02:01.993
"so fun" --

00:02:01.993 --> 00:02:05.080
you're going to notice
there's something much deeper going on.

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:08.667
I've collected thousands of these stories
from people all around the world,

00:02:08.708 --> 00:02:10.251
and I can tell you it's amazing,

00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:14.964
because when people recount the memories
in which they had the most fun,

00:02:14.964 --> 00:02:17.676
they tell you about some
of the most joyful and treasured

00:02:17.717 --> 00:02:19.928
memories of their lives.

00:02:20.845 --> 00:02:24.766
So in reality, fun is not
just lighthearted pleasure.

00:02:25.392 --> 00:02:26.685
It's not just for kids,

00:02:26.685 --> 00:02:29.312
and it is definitely not frivolous.

00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:33.775
Instead, fun is the secret
to feeling alive.

00:02:35.151 --> 00:02:36.903
So today, I want to propose to you

00:02:36.903 --> 00:02:40.198
a new, more precise definition
of what fun is.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.202
I want to reveal some of the ways
in which it is astonishingly good for us,

00:02:44.202 --> 00:02:46.246
and I want to give you all
some suggestions

00:02:46.287 --> 00:02:50.959
for things you can do starting right now
to experience its power for yourself.

00:02:51.918 --> 00:02:53.878
So the first thing we need to start with

00:02:53.878 --> 00:02:57.298
is the fact that fun is a feeling,
and it's not an activity.

00:02:57.674 --> 00:02:59.175
And that's important,

00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:02.220
because a lot of times,
when I ask people what's fun,

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:04.973
they'll respond with a list
of activities that they enjoy.

00:03:05.014 --> 00:03:08.852
You know, they'll say, "Dancing is fun,"
or "Skiing is fun," or, I don't know,

00:03:08.852 --> 00:03:10.770
"Pickleball is fun."

00:03:11.604 --> 00:03:14.941
Everyone seems to think
that pickleball is fun.

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:17.986
(Cheers and laughter)

00:03:18.027 --> 00:03:20.280
And sure, pickleball can be fun,

00:03:20.321 --> 00:03:22.782
but we've all had experiences
where something's off,

00:03:22.824 --> 00:03:25.034
and an activity that seems
like it would be fun

00:03:25.034 --> 00:03:26.661
doesn't end up feeling fun.

00:03:26.703 --> 00:03:27.954
And then on the flip side,

00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:31.791
we've had experiences where something
that doesn't seem like it'll be fun at all

00:03:31.791 --> 00:03:33.418
ends up feeling ridiculously fun.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.253
There's an element of serendipity.

00:03:35.295 --> 00:03:37.172
But when people do have fun,

00:03:37.172 --> 00:03:39.007
when they experience this feeling,

00:03:39.007 --> 00:03:40.800
it's actually very easy to recognize,

00:03:40.842 --> 00:03:42.510
because people who are having fun

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.513
look like they're being
illuminated from within.

00:03:46.431 --> 00:03:47.599
So, for example,

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:49.934
here is me and my husband
having fun together.

00:03:50.894 --> 00:03:53.104
Here are some presidents
having fun together.

00:03:53.146 --> 00:03:55.398
(Laughter)

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.985
Here’s Archbishop Desmond Tutu
and the Dalai Lama having fun together.

00:03:58.985 --> 00:04:00.487
(Cheers and laughter)

00:04:00.487 --> 00:04:05.283
Actually, those two seemed like
they were very often, even constantly ...

00:04:05.325 --> 00:04:07.035
(Laughter)

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:08.453
... having fun together.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.706
(Laughter)

00:04:11.748 --> 00:04:13.708
And as you can see in these photographs,

00:04:13.708 --> 00:04:17.545
true fun produces this visceral sense
of lightness and joy.

00:04:18.046 --> 00:04:19.672
It's radiant.

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:22.967
In fact, when I asked my daughter,
when she was about five years old,

00:04:23.009 --> 00:04:24.761
what color fun would be,

00:04:24.761 --> 00:04:26.221
she said "sunshine."

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:29.933
So what is this sunshine?

00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:32.268
You know? What is this feeling
that we call "fun"?

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.437
When people tell me
their stories about fun,

00:04:34.437 --> 00:04:37.440
it's really interesting,
because the details are all different,

00:04:37.482 --> 00:04:38.691
and often quite mundane,

00:04:38.691 --> 00:04:41.027
but the energy running
through them is the same.

00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:43.696
There were three factors
that are consistently present,

00:04:43.696 --> 00:04:46.616
to the point that I believe
they constitute a new definition,

00:04:46.658 --> 00:04:49.619
one that is a lot more accurate
than what's in the dictionary.

00:04:49.661 --> 00:04:53.248
And those three factors
are playfulness, connection and flow.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:55.416
So by playfulness,

00:04:55.458 --> 00:04:57.752
I do not mean you have to play games,

00:04:57.794 --> 00:05:00.296
or, God forbid, make believe.

00:05:00.922 --> 00:05:03.466
I just mean having a lighthearted attitude

00:05:03.508 --> 00:05:05.635
of doing things
for the sake of doing them

00:05:05.635 --> 00:05:07.720
and not caring too much about the outcome.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.097
Letting go of perfectionism.

00:05:09.097 --> 00:05:11.099
When we have fun, our guard is down,

00:05:11.099 --> 00:05:13.393
and we're not taking ourselves
too seriously.

00:05:14.102 --> 00:05:17.897
Connection refers to the feeling of having
a special, shared experience.

00:05:17.897 --> 00:05:20.400
And I do think it's possible,
in some circumstances,

00:05:20.441 --> 00:05:21.776
to have fun alone,

00:05:21.776 --> 00:05:25.363
and for this feeling of connection
to be with yourself or the surroundings,

00:05:25.363 --> 00:05:26.531
or the activity.

00:05:26.573 --> 00:05:28.992
But in the majority of stories
that people tell me

00:05:28.992 --> 00:05:30.535
about their peak fun memories,

00:05:30.577 --> 00:05:32.203
another person is involved.

00:05:32.203 --> 00:05:33.997
And that's true even for introverts.

00:05:34.831 --> 00:05:37.876
And then flow is the state
where we are so engaged and focused

00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:39.252
on whatever we're doing

00:05:39.294 --> 00:05:41.087
that we can even lose track of time.

00:05:41.087 --> 00:05:43.715
You can think about an athlete
in the middle of a game,

00:05:43.756 --> 00:05:45.675
or a musician playing a piece of music.

00:05:45.675 --> 00:05:47.051
It's when we're in the zone.

00:05:47.051 --> 00:05:49.888
It's possible to be in flow
and not have fun,

00:05:49.888 --> 00:05:51.139
like if you're arguing,

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:53.808
but you cannot have fun
if you're not in flow.

00:05:54.809 --> 00:05:57.854
So playfulness, connection and flow
all feel great on their own.

00:05:57.854 --> 00:06:00.106
But when we experience all three at once,

00:06:00.148 --> 00:06:01.983
something magical happens.

00:06:02.317 --> 00:06:03.818
We have fun.

00:06:04.527 --> 00:06:06.279
And that doesn't just feel good,

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:08.323
it is good for us.

00:06:08.323 --> 00:06:10.992
In fact, fun does so many
amazingly good things for us

00:06:11.034 --> 00:06:15.121
that I personally believe that fun is not
just the result of human thriving,

00:06:15.163 --> 00:06:16.331
it's a cause.

00:06:17.081 --> 00:06:19.709
So, for example, fun is energizing.

00:06:20.585 --> 00:06:23.755
When people tell me their stories
about fun, they glow.

00:06:23.755 --> 00:06:26.132
It is like a fire has been lit
inside of them,

00:06:26.132 --> 00:06:29.552
and the energy and the warmth
that they give off is contagious.

00:06:29.594 --> 00:06:31.346
You know, so much of life drains us,

00:06:31.346 --> 00:06:32.972
but fun fills us up.

00:06:33.973 --> 00:06:35.892
Fun also makes us present.

00:06:35.934 --> 00:06:39.020
A lot of us put a lot of work
into trying to be more present --

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:41.940
we do yoga classes, we meditate,
and that is all great,

00:06:41.981 --> 00:06:45.610
but the fact that fun is a flow state
means that when we are having fun,

00:06:45.652 --> 00:06:47.737
we simply are present.

00:06:47.737 --> 00:06:49.614
There's no other way for it to happen.

00:06:50.865 --> 00:06:52.367
Fun also unites us.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.452
We live in a really polarized world,

00:06:54.494 --> 00:06:57.288
and as we all know,
there's a lot of very serious problems.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.707
But when we have fun with people,

00:06:59.749 --> 00:07:02.085
we don't see them
as different political parties,

00:07:02.085 --> 00:07:03.628
or nationalities or religions.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.798
We connect with them as human beings,

00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:09.175
and it's worth noting
that that is the first step

00:07:09.217 --> 00:07:12.053
in being able to work together
to solve those problems.

00:07:13.304 --> 00:07:15.431
Fun also makes us healthier.

00:07:15.473 --> 00:07:17.058
Being lonely and stressed out,

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.436
as many of us have been
for the past two years,

00:07:20.436 --> 00:07:24.273
causes hormonal changes in our bodies
that increase our risks for disease.

00:07:24.315 --> 00:07:25.858
But when we have fun,

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.277
we're relaxed and we're more
socially connected,

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:30.905
both of which have the opposite effect.

00:07:31.322 --> 00:07:34.325
So, kind of blows my mind every time
I think about it this way,

00:07:34.367 --> 00:07:37.036
but having fun is a health intervention.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:39.956
And then, lastly, fun is joyful.

00:07:39.998 --> 00:07:42.333
You know, we all so desperately
want to be happy.

00:07:42.375 --> 00:07:43.876
We read books about happiness,

00:07:43.876 --> 00:07:45.503
we download apps about happiness,

00:07:45.503 --> 00:07:48.423
but when we are in a moment
of having fun, we are happy.

00:07:49.549 --> 00:07:51.300
So it makes me think that, perhaps,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:52.969
the secret to long-term happiness

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:55.221
is just to have more
everyday moments of fun.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.223
So how do we do that?

00:07:57.223 --> 00:07:58.808
How do we have more fun?

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.184
Well, to start with,

00:08:00.226 --> 00:08:03.438
do not take the suggestions
you'll find in magazine articles

00:08:03.438 --> 00:08:04.772
about how to have more fun.

00:08:04.814 --> 00:08:06.566
I looked at some of these myself,

00:08:06.607 --> 00:08:09.986
and I found suggestions that include --
and I'm not making these up --

00:08:09.986 --> 00:08:11.237
"roast a turkey."

00:08:11.279 --> 00:08:14.240
(Laughter)

00:08:14.282 --> 00:08:16.826
"Put together an altar
to loved ones who have passed."

00:08:16.826 --> 00:08:19.996
(Laughter)

00:08:20.455 --> 00:08:23.291
“Watch a documentary
about climate change.”

00:08:23.332 --> 00:08:26.878
(Laughter)

00:08:26.878 --> 00:08:28.212
And my personal favorite,

00:08:28.254 --> 00:08:29.922
"Adorn your table with gourds."

00:08:29.964 --> 00:08:31.340
(Laughter)

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.678
Those are not good suggestions.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.681
Instead, the most effective thing
you can do to have more fun

00:08:38.681 --> 00:08:41.225
is to focus on its ingredients,
by which I mean,

00:08:41.225 --> 00:08:44.729
do everything you can to fill your life
with more moments of playfulness,

00:08:44.729 --> 00:08:46.272
connection and flow.

00:08:46.272 --> 00:08:48.191
So here are some ideas for how to do so.

00:08:48.232 --> 00:08:52.070
To start with, reduce distractions
in order to increase flow.

00:08:52.111 --> 00:08:54.947
Anything that distracts you
is going to kick you out of flow

00:08:54.947 --> 00:08:56.657
and prevent you from having fun.

00:08:56.699 --> 00:08:58.951
And what's the number one
source of distraction

00:08:58.951 --> 00:09:00.745
for most of us, these days?

00:09:00.787 --> 00:09:01.954
Oh, thank you.

00:09:01.996 --> 00:09:02.997
(Laughs)

00:09:03.039 --> 00:09:04.999
It was rhetorical, but yes, your phones.

00:09:04.999 --> 00:09:06.000
(Laughs)

00:09:06.042 --> 00:09:09.253
I wrote a book called
"How to Break Up With Your Phone,"

00:09:09.295 --> 00:09:11.089
so I have strong feelings about this,

00:09:11.089 --> 00:09:13.841
but I can guarantee you
that you are not going to have fun

00:09:13.841 --> 00:09:16.219
if you're constantly on your phone.

00:09:16.219 --> 00:09:19.764
So today, I want to challenge you
to keep your phone out of your hand

00:09:19.806 --> 00:09:20.973
as much as possible,

00:09:20.973 --> 00:09:23.184
so you can take me up
on my second suggestion,

00:09:23.184 --> 00:09:24.769
which is to increase connection

00:09:24.811 --> 00:09:27.605
by interacting more
with other human beings in real life.

00:09:27.647 --> 00:09:31.192
Now, I know that one of the main reasons
we're constantly on our phones

00:09:31.192 --> 00:09:34.487
is specifically to avoid
having to spend time

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:36.906
and interact with other
human beings in real life.

00:09:36.948 --> 00:09:37.949
(Laughter)

00:09:37.949 --> 00:09:40.493
So I want to assure you
that it is worth it,

00:09:40.535 --> 00:09:42.787
and it is not as hard as it might seem.

00:09:42.829 --> 00:09:44.288
So here's how you do it.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.457
You start by making
eye contact with someone.

00:09:46.457 --> 00:09:47.834
Like, look them in the eye,

00:09:47.834 --> 00:09:49.919
don't look in the middle
of their forehead,

00:09:49.919 --> 00:09:51.921
where the camera would be on a Zoom call.

00:09:51.921 --> 00:09:53.131
(Laughter)

00:09:53.131 --> 00:09:54.966
And you say “Hello.”

00:09:55.007 --> 00:09:58.136
And if that goes well,
you can introduce yourself.

00:09:58.469 --> 00:09:59.637
And if that goes well,

00:09:59.679 --> 00:10:02.974
maybe you can ask them a question,
something that's thought-provoking,

00:10:03.015 --> 00:10:04.934
but not overly personal or threatening,

00:10:04.934 --> 00:10:07.103
like "What's something
that fascinates you?"

00:10:07.145 --> 00:10:09.564
Or "What's one thing
that delighted you today?"

00:10:10.273 --> 00:10:14.235
And you might be amazed by how good
just one little moment of connection

00:10:14.277 --> 00:10:15.528
can make you feel.

00:10:16.362 --> 00:10:18.364
And if you do find someone
to connect with,

00:10:18.364 --> 00:10:21.075
maybe ask them to join you
in trying my third suggestion,

00:10:21.075 --> 00:10:25.037
which is to increase playfulness
by finding opportunities to rebel.

00:10:25.997 --> 00:10:29.083
Now I am not talking about
James Dean-level of rebellion.

00:10:29.083 --> 00:10:31.586
I'm talking about playful deviance.

00:10:31.586 --> 00:10:33.921
I'm talking about finding ways
to break the rules

00:10:33.963 --> 00:10:35.256
of responsible adulthood,

00:10:35.256 --> 00:10:39.051
and giving yourself permission
to get a kick out of your own life.

00:10:39.635 --> 00:10:41.095
One person told me

00:10:41.095 --> 00:10:43.598
that some of the most fun
she'd had in recent memory,

00:10:43.639 --> 00:10:45.057
happened on a Friday morning,

00:10:45.057 --> 00:10:47.476
when she and some of her friends
ditched their work

00:10:47.476 --> 00:10:49.312
and their childcare responsibilities,

00:10:49.353 --> 00:10:51.189
tucked flasks into their purses

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:56.027
and snuck out to a 10:30am showing
of the movie "Bad Moms."

00:10:56.027 --> 00:11:00.031
(Laughter)

00:11:00.031 --> 00:11:02.700
So lastly, here's one more thing
that you can do today

00:11:02.742 --> 00:11:04.577
to start having more fun.

00:11:06.037 --> 00:11:07.705
[Roast a Turkey]
I am just kidding.

00:11:07.705 --> 00:11:09.040
(Laughter)

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.374
Prioritize it.

00:11:10.416 --> 00:11:12.001
That might sound totally obvious,

00:11:12.043 --> 00:11:14.629
but one of the main reasons
we're not having enough fun

00:11:14.629 --> 00:11:16.505
is that we're not making it a priority.

00:11:16.505 --> 00:11:18.591
Our fun is always
at the bottom of the list,

00:11:18.633 --> 00:11:20.218
and it can't speak up for itself.

00:11:20.218 --> 00:11:23.596
So I'm not suggesting that you take out
your calendar and make an entry

00:11:23.638 --> 00:11:27.016
that says: “From 4 to 6pm
on Saturday, I shall have fun.”

00:11:27.016 --> 00:11:29.352
That is a guaranteed way to not have fun.

00:11:29.769 --> 00:11:32.146
But if you know you consistently have fun

00:11:32.188 --> 00:11:34.440
when you spend time
with a particular person,

00:11:34.482 --> 00:11:36.609
make a point to spend time
with that person.

00:11:36.651 --> 00:11:38.152
If you know there's an activity

00:11:38.194 --> 00:11:41.948
that really does often generate
playful connected flow for you,

00:11:41.948 --> 00:11:44.158
carve out time for it in your schedule.

00:11:44.659 --> 00:11:46.827
Treat fun as if it is important.

00:11:46.827 --> 00:11:48.162
Because it is.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.958
I've been doing this myself
for a couple of years now,

00:11:51.958 --> 00:11:55.127
and it's amazing to see how many areas
of my life fun has touched.

00:11:55.169 --> 00:11:59.257
I'm more creative and more productive,
I'm more resilient.

00:11:59.298 --> 00:12:00.424
I laugh more.

00:12:00.883 --> 00:12:04.512
Making sure that I'm having enough fun
has made me a better partner,

00:12:04.553 --> 00:12:06.931
a better parent and a better friend.

00:12:07.848 --> 00:12:09.892
And it has convinced me of something

00:12:09.934 --> 00:12:13.271
that I very much hope
I can convince you of as well,

00:12:13.312 --> 00:12:15.523
which is that my daughter was right.

00:12:16.148 --> 00:12:18.150
Fun is sunshine.

00:12:18.776 --> 00:12:21.237
It's a distillation of life's energy.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.115
And the more often we experience it,

00:12:24.115 --> 00:12:27.118
the more we will feel
like we're actually alive.

00:12:27.576 --> 00:12:28.703
Thank you.

00:12:28.744 --> 00:12:33.791
(Cheers and applause)

